__d(function(e,o,r,a,t,n,i){t.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 Social and Political Life",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 I (Textbook for Class VI)",chapter_number:29,chapter_title:"Urban Livelihoods",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does the term 'livelihood' mean?",options:["A) The place where a person lives","B) The way people earn money or resources to meet their daily needs","C) The type of house a person owns in a city","D) The number of family members a person supports"],correct_answer:"B) The way people earn money or resources to meet their daily needs",explanation:"A livelihood is the way people earn money or obtain resources to meet their basic needs \u2014 food, clothing, shelter, and education. In cities, people earn their livelihoods in many different ways, from factory work to street vending to professional services."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is an example of an organised sector worker?",options:["A) A vegetable vendor selling from a cart","B) A construction labourer hired daily by a contractor","C) A government school teacher with a fixed salary and Provident Fund","D) A cycle-rickshaw puller who earns per ride"],correct_answer:"C) A government school teacher with a fixed salary and Provident Fund",explanation:"A government school teacher is an organised sector worker \u2014 they have a written contract, fixed monthly salary, paid leave, Provident Fund, and job security under government service rules. The other options are all unorganised or self-employed workers with no such protections."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Approximately what percentage of India's workforce is in the unorganised sector?",options:["A) 10%","B) 30%","C) 60%","D) 90%"],correct_answer:"D) 90%",explanation:"About 90% of India's workforce is in the unorganised sector \u2014 approximately 400\u2013450 million workers who have no written contracts, no fixed salaries, no Provident Fund, and no job security. This makes improving conditions for unorganised workers one of India's most pressing social challenges."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"A Provident Fund (PF) is:",options:["A) A daily wage paid to construction workers","B) A retirement savings scheme where both employee and employer contribute monthly","C) A government scheme to provide food to urban poor workers","D) A fine paid by employers for violating labour laws"],correct_answer:"B) A retirement savings scheme where both employee and employer contribute monthly",explanation:"A Provident Fund (PF) is a retirement savings scheme for organised sector workers. Both the employee and employer contribute a fixed percentage of the salary every month. The accumulated amount is given to the worker on retirement. Only organised sector workers have PF; unorganised workers have no such retirement safety net."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following workers is self-employed?",options:["A) A police officer in a government station","B) An auto-rickshaw driver who owns his vehicle and keeps his fares","C) A factory worker who gets a fixed monthly salary","D) A bank manager who gets annual bonuses"],correct_answer:"B) An auto-rickshaw driver who owns his vehicle and keeps his fares",explanation:"A self-employed person works for themselves \u2014 they have no fixed employer and earn based on how much work they do. An auto-rickshaw driver who owns his vehicle is self-employed: he pays for his own fuel, maintenance, and permit, and keeps his daily fare earnings. Police officers, factory workers, and bank managers all work for employers."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why do many workers migrate from villages to cities?",options:["A) Because cities have better weather than villages","B) Because farming is illegal in cities","C) Because cities offer more types of jobs and higher wages than farming villages","D) Because the government forces villagers to move to cities"],correct_answer:"C) Because cities offer more types of jobs and higher wages than farming villages",explanation:"People migrate from villages to cities because cities offer more diverse employment opportunities and higher wages \u2014 even for unskilled work \u2014 than seasonal farming. Migrant workers typically work in construction, domestic service, street vending, and transport. They often send remittances back to support their families in the village."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is a 'remittance' in the context of urban livelihoods?",options:["A) A tax paid by workers to the city government","B) Money sent by a migrant city worker to their family back home","C) A bonus given by employers to factory workers","D) A fine charged to street vendors for vending without a licence"],correct_answer:"B) Money sent by a migrant city worker to their family back home",explanation:"A remittance is money sent by a migrant worker (working in a city or abroad) to their family in their home village or town. Remittances are often the primary source of cash income for rural families. They pay for children's education, medical expenses, food, and home construction."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is NOT a feature of the unorganised sector?",options:["A) Daily or weekly wages instead of fixed monthly salary","B) No written employment contract","C) Provident Fund deducted every month","D) No paid leave or medical benefits"],correct_answer:"C) Provident Fund deducted every month",explanation:"Provident Fund (PF) is a feature of the ORGANISED sector \u2014 it is deducted monthly from organised workers' salaries and matched by their employers. Unorganised sector workers have no PF. The other options \u2014 daily wages, no written contract, no paid leave \u2014 are all characteristics of the unorganised sector."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The concept of 'Dignity of Labour' means:",options:["A) Only skilled and well-paid work deserves respect","B) All types of work, regardless of pay or social status, are equally important and deserve respect","C) Workers in the organised sector are more dignified than unorganised workers","D) Only government jobs carry dignity"],correct_answer:"B) All types of work, regardless of pay or social status, are equally important and deserve respect",explanation:"Dignity of Labour is the principle that all kinds of work \u2014 sweeping, construction, farming, professional work \u2014 are equally valuable and deserve equal respect. Without ragpickers, sanitation workers, and construction labourers, cities could not function. Every worker contributes to society and deserves respect regardless of their wage or job type."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"A construction labourer is paid \u20b9500 only on days when he actually works. This is an example of:",options:["A) A monthly salary","B) Provident Fund","C) A daily wage","D) A remittance"],correct_answer:"C) A daily wage",explanation:"A daily wage is payment for work calculated per day \u2014 the worker is paid only on days they actually work. Construction labourers, casual farm workers, and many unorganised sector workers receive daily wages. If they are sick, it rains, or there is no work, they earn nothing that day \u2014 unlike salaried workers who receive a fixed monthly payment."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Harpreet works in an IT company with a fixed salary, paid leave, and Provident Fund. Vandana runs a small flower shop near a temple and earns based on daily sales. Which statement CORRECTLY describes their situations?",options:["A) Both Harpreet and Vandana are in the organised sector","B) Harpreet is in the organised sector; Vandana is self-employed in the unorganised sector","C) Both are in the unorganised sector since neither works for the government","D) Vandana is in the organised sector because she works every day"],correct_answer:"B) Harpreet is in the organised sector; Vandana is self-employed in the unorganised sector",explanation:"Harpreet has all the features of the organised sector: fixed salary, paid leave, and Provident Fund \u2014 from a registered private company (IT firms are registered businesses covered by labour laws). Vandana is self-employed and in the unorganised sector: she has no fixed employer, earns based on daily sales, has no paid leave or PF, and can have zero income on a bad day. Being in the organised sector is not limited to government jobs."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Sudha works in a garment factory. She has a fixed monthly wage of \u20b910,000 but has no Provident Fund and gets only 6 days of paid leave per year. Which sector does she most likely belong to?",options:["A) Clearly organised sector \u2014 she has a fixed wage","B) Clearly unorganised sector \u2014 she works in a factory","C) Semi-organised \u2014 she has some features of the organised sector (fixed wage) but lacks full protections (no PF)","D) Self-employed sector \u2014 all factory workers are self-employed"],correct_answer:"C) Semi-organised \u2014 she has some features of the organised sector (fixed wage) but lacks full protections (no PF)",explanation:"Sudha's situation is common for workers in small and medium factories in India. She has a fixed monthly wage (organised feature) but lacks full legal protections like Provident Fund and adequate paid leave (unorganised features). This is called the 'semi-organised' or 'informal organised' sector \u2014 workers are somewhat regular but do not receive all mandated benefits."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following correctly describes why domestic workers (maids, cooks) are in the unorganised sector?",options:["A) Because they work inside homes and not in factories","B) Because they have no written contract, no PF, no ESI, and can be dismissed without notice","C) Because they only work part-time","D) Because domestic work is not considered a real job under Indian law"],correct_answer:"B) Because they have no written contract, no PF, no ESI, and can be dismissed without notice",explanation:"Domestic workers are in the unorganised sector because of the nature of their employment \u2014 no written contract, no Provident Fund, no Employees' State Insurance (ESI), no job security, and no legal minimum wage enforcement in practice. They may work for the same household for years but remain legally unprotected. The location of work (home vs factory) is not the defining factor \u2014 the absence of legal protections is."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"In Bengaluru, IT professionals earn lakhs per month while construction workers who build the same offices earn \u20b9500 per day. What does this tell us about urban livelihoods?",options:["A) IT professionals work harder than construction workers","B) Construction work is less important than IT work","C) There is significant income inequality between organised and unorganised sector workers in the same city","D) Construction workers choose to earn less because they prefer outdoor work"],correct_answer:"C) There is significant income inequality between organised and unorganised sector workers in the same city",explanation:"The vast gap between IT professional salaries and construction worker daily wages reflects deep income inequality in urban India. Both groups work hard and are essential to the city's functioning \u2014 but sector, education, skills, and job security create enormous differences in income. This inequality is a major social challenge. Neither group 'chooses' their income \u2014 it is shaped by education access, family background, and economic structures."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The Street Vendors (Protection of Livelihood and Regulation of Street Vending) Act was passed in India in 2014. What is the main purpose of this Act?",options:["A) To remove all street vendors from Indian cities","B) To give street vendors the right to vend in designated areas and protect their livelihood","C) To tax street vendors heavily to fund city development","D) To force street vendors to set up permanent shops"],correct_answer:"B) To give street vendors the right to vend in designated areas and protect their livelihood",explanation:"The Street Vendors Act (2014) recognises street vending as a legitimate livelihood and gives vendors legal protection. Cities must create Town Vending Committees and issue certificates to registered vendors, giving them the right to vend in designated spaces without being harassed by police or municipality officials. This law recognises the dignity of street vendors' work and their right to earn a living."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Employees' State Insurance (ESI) provides which of the following benefits to organised sector workers?",options:["A) Free housing in government quarters","B) Medical treatment, cash during sick leave, maternity benefit, and injury compensation","C) Free education for their children in private schools","D) A government pension after 10 years of service"],correct_answer:"B) Medical treatment, cash during sick leave, maternity benefit, and injury compensation",explanation:"ESI (Employees' State Insurance) is a social security scheme covering medical treatment for the worker and family, cash benefit during sick leave, maternity benefit for female workers, disablement benefit if injured at work, and dependent benefit if a worker dies in a work accident. It covers organised sector workers in registered establishments earning below a certain income threshold."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following best explains why a street vendor has MORE freedom but LESS security than a salaried government employee?",options:["A) The vendor is richer and can afford to take risks","B) The vendor has no fixed employer so they choose their own hours and location, but earn nothing on bad days and have no PF, leave, or job protection","C) The government employee is lazy, which is why they need job security","D) Freedom and security are always equal \u2014 you cannot have more of one without having more of the other"],correct_answer:"B) The vendor has no fixed employer so they choose their own hours and location, but earn nothing on bad days and have no PF, leave, or job protection",explanation:"Self-employment gives freedom (no boss, flexible hours, independent decision-making) but removes security (no fixed income, no paid leave, no PF, no legal protection). A salaried government employee has less flexibility but enjoys job security, fixed monthly income, paid leave, PF, and pension. This trade-off between freedom and security is a key feature of self-employment vs. organised employment."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Mysuru is famous for its silk sarees. The weavers who make these sarees are mostly:",options:["A) Organised sector factory workers earning fixed salaries with full benefits","B) Government employees under the Karnataka Silk Industries Corporation","C) Self-employed artisans or cooperative members earning based on pieces produced, often in the unorganised sector","D) IT professionals who have shifted to silk weaving as a hobby"],correct_answer:"C) Self-employed artisans or cooperative members earning based on pieces produced, often in the unorganised sector",explanation:"Mysuru's silk weavers are predominantly self-employed artisans or work through cooperatives (like Karnataka Silk Industries Corporation for some). Many work from home or in small units, earning per piece woven. While some government-supported weavers have slightly better protections, most handloom weavers are in the unorganised sector \u2014 their income is irregular, they have no PF, and their earnings depend on how many sarees they complete and the price they can negotiate."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A student argues: 'If unorganised sector workers form trade unions, their problems will be solved.' Evaluate this statement.",options:["A) Completely correct \u2014 trade unions always solve all worker problems instantly","B) Partially correct \u2014 trade unions can help workers bargain for better wages and conditions, but unorganised workers face unique challenges (scattered employment, migrant status, no single employer) that make union formation difficult","C) Completely incorrect \u2014 trade unions are only for factory workers and cannot help street vendors","D) Completely incorrect \u2014 the government has already solved all problems of unorganised workers through laws"],correct_answer:"B) Partially correct \u2014 trade unions can help workers bargain for better wages and conditions, but unorganised workers face unique challenges (scattered employment, migrant status, no single employer) that make union formation difficult",explanation:"Trade unions can empower workers to collectively bargain for better wages, safer conditions, and legal rights. However, unorganised sector workers face specific obstacles: they work for many different employers (or no employer), are often migrants who fear deportation or job loss, lack awareness of their rights, and are geographically scattered. Still, examples like SEWA (Self Employed Women's Association) in India show that organising unorganised workers IS possible and effective. The statement is partially correct but oversimplified."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"Bachchu Manjhi is a cycle-rickshaw puller (migrant from Bihar) and Radhika is an IT engineer in the same city. They both contribute to city life. Which of the following statements BEST captures the social lesson this chapter teaches?",options:["A) Radhika is more important to the city than Bachchu because she earns more","B) Bachchu should go back to his village because the city doesn't need low-paid workers","C) Both Bachchu and Radhika contribute to city life; the enormous gap in their income and security reflects social inequality that must be recognised and addressed","D) Both earn fairly \u2014 their wages perfectly reflect how hard each works"],correct_answer:"C) Both Bachchu and Radhika contribute to city life; the enormous gap in their income and security reflects social inequality that must be recognised and addressed",explanation:"The chapter's core message is that all urban workers \u2014 regardless of income, sector, or type of work \u2014 contribute to city life and deserve dignity and recognition. The vast inequality between a cycle-rickshaw puller (unorganised, migrant, daily wage) and an IT engineer (organised, high salary, full benefits) doing equally essential work reflects deep structural inequalities in access to education, opportunity, and legal protections. Recognising this inequality is the first step toward addressing it \u2014 through better labour laws, social security for all, and changing social attitudes."}]}},2755,[]);